Rheumatic Heart Disease
Cardiovascular
Mid-diastolic rumbling murmur, Loud S1/Opening snap, History of rheumatic fever, Mitral valve calcification/fusion

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CDV-037
Reason for Visit: Progressive, severe shortness of breath at rest and an episode of coughing up blood-tinged sputum.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and adult daughter
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a patient with a known history of childhood rheumatic fever experiencing worsening dyspnea and new-onset hemoptysis. This presentation points to acute decompensation of severe mitral stenosis leading to pulmonary hypertension or impending pulmonary edema. Urgent physical evaluation was required to assess respiratory compromise, rule out an acute embolic event, and initiate immediate diuretic therapy, preventing respiratory failure before routine in-hours care could be accessed.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: "I've been getting more and more breathless over the years, but tonight I can't catch my breath at all and I just coughed up some pink spit."
History of Present Illness: Patient (aged 68) reports a slowly progressive exertional dyspnea over decades, which has acutely deteriorated over the past 48 hours. Now experiencing breathlessness with minimal exertion and orthopnea. Tonight, the patient experienced a brief episode of exertional hemoptysis (blood-tinged sputum) following a coughing fit. Reports severe generalized fatigue and a sensation of an irregular, rapid heartbeat over the last few days. Patient has a confirmed history of acute rheumatic fever during childhood. KEY SYMPTOMS: History of acute rheumatic fever or recurrent severe childhood pharyngitis. Slowly progressive exertional dyspnoea over decades. Exertional haemoptysis.
Functional Status: Highly restricted; currently struggling to complete full sentences without pausing for breath.
Social/Safety Concerns: High risk of acute respiratory decline; daughter staying overnight to monitor.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 110/70 | HR: 115 (Irregular) | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 24 | SpO2: 92 percent (on room air) | Wt: 75 Kg
General: Visibly dyspneic, sitting upright, utilizing accessory muscles. A distinct plum-red discoloration of the cheeks (malar flush) is noted.
Physical Exam: Cardiovascular: Irregularly irregular rhythm indicating new or poorly controlled atrial fibrillation. A loud, snapping first heart sound (S1) is auscultated. This is followed by a high-pitched opening snap just after the second heart sound (S2), leading into a low-pitched, mid-diastolic rumbling murmur heard best at the apex with the bell in the left lateral decubitus position. Respiratory: Fine bibasal crackles audible on deep inspiration. Extremities: Mild bilateral ankle edema. DIAGNOSTIC TETRAD: Mid-diastolic rumbling murmur, Loud S1/Opening snap, History of rheumatic fever, Mitral valve calcification/fusion (anticipated on formal echocardiogram).
Environmental Observations: Multiple pillows stacked on the bed for orthopnea.
Medication Reconciliation: Compliant with long-term low-dose diuretics; no current anticoagulation.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Rheumatic Heart Disease (Severe Mitral Stenosis with Acute Decompensation and Atrial Fibrillation) - Status: Exacerbated (High Risk)
Differentials:

Acute Pulmonary Embolism - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The presence of the classic structural auscultatory triad (loud S1, opening snap, mid-diastolic rumble) and a decades-long history of progressive dyspnea, securely anchoring the hemoptysis and dyspnea to severe mitral valve obstruction and pulmonary venous hypertension.

Mitral Regurgitation - Ruled out/unlikely due to: The murmur is a mid-diastolic rumble with a loud S1, rather than a pansystolic blowing murmur radiating to the axilla with a soft S1.

Lower Respiratory Tract Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Absence of fever or purulent sputum, and the presence of new-onset atrial fibrillation, malar flush, and specific valvular pathology causing cardiogenic pulmonary congestion.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The patient presents with the hallmark signs of severe, decompensated rheumatic mitral stenosis: a loud S1, opening snap, mid-diastolic rumble, malar flush, and a history of rheumatic fever. The new-onset hemoptysis, resting tachypnea, and rapid atrial fibrillation indicate acute pulmonary venous hypertension and impending frank pulmonary edema. This represents a critical, life-threatening cardiopulmonary failure. Delaying care until routine hours was absolutely contraindicated. Urgent after-hours physical assessment was mandatory to clinically confirm the structural decompensation, administer immediate intravenous diuresis, and arrange emergency hospital transfer for rate control and definitive valvular management.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Administered Furosemide 40mg IV stat to reduce pulmonary venous congestion. Provided supplemental oxygen via nasal cannula. Arranged immediate emergency ambulance transfer to the hospital for continuous monitoring, rate control of the atrial fibrillation, and urgent cardiothoracic review.
Positive Behavioral Change: Instructed the patient to remain seated strictly upright at 90 degrees with legs dangling over the side of the bed while awaiting the ambulance. By enforcing this position, gravity traps fluid in the lower extremities, actively reducing the venous return to the obstructed heart. The reward is a rapid, physical reduction in the feeling of drowning and breathlessness.
Home-Based Services: None.
DME/Safety: None.
Follow-up: Acute hospital admission expected. Specialized cardiothoracic intervention (e.g., valvuloplasty or replacement) likely required.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Ambulance dispatched. If the patient becomes severely confused, if the breathing becomes agonizing, or if they cough up copious amounts of frothy pink fluid, the daughter must call 000 immediately for a priority upgrade.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Diagnosis relies on classic acoustic signs; definitive assessment of valve calcification requires a hospital echocardiogram; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The childhood infection permanently scarred the heart valve, which has now become critically narrowed. Hospitalization is required to clear the fluid from the lungs and evaluate if the valve can be repaired.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Stay sitting upright with your legs hanging down. Do not attempt to walk. Let the paramedics handle the transfer.

